 # Span Space Decomposition
-
 Also called: span space techniques
-
 ## Overview
-Span space decomposition is a family of isosurface extraction methods that partition volume cells by mapping their extreme scalar values into a 2D coordinate system. It solves the problem of efficiently identifying cells intersecting an isosurface by reducing the search to a 2D range query, achieving a worst-case search complexity of $O(\sqrt{n} + k)$, where $n$ is the dataset size and $k$ is the number of cells in the isosurface [S1]. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly leveraging the geometric properties of min/max value pairs in a 2D plane rather than relying solely on hierarchical spatial subdivision of the domain.
-
+Span space decomposition is a family of isosurface extraction methods that partition volume cells by mapping their extreme scalar values into a 2D coordinate system [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently identifying cells intersecting an isosurface by reducing the search to a 2D range query [S1]. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly leveraging the geometric properties of min/max value pairs in a 2D plane rather than relying solely on hierarchical spatial subdivision of the domain [G].
 ## Shared principles
-The core mechanism of span space decomposition involves mapping each volume cell to a point in a 2D space based on its scalar field extrema.
-
+The core mechanism involves mapping each volume cell to a point in a 2D space based on its scalar field extrema [S1].
 ### Mapping
-Each cell $c$ in the volume is mapped to a point $P_c = (x, y)$ in span space, defined as:
-$$ x = \min_{v \in c} f(v) $$
-$$ y = \max_{v \in c} f(v) $$
-where $f(v)$ is the scalar value at vertex $v$. By definition, for all cells, $y \ge x$, meaning all points lie on or above the line $y = x$ [S1].
-
+Each cell $c$ is mapped to a point $P_c = (x, y)$ in span space, where $x = \min_{v \in c} f(v)$ and $y = \max_{v \in c} f(v)$ [S1]. By definition, $y \ge x$, so all points lie on or above the line $y = x$ [S1].
 ### Intersection Condition
-For a given isovalue $v$, a cell intersects the isosurface if and only if the isovalue lies strictly between the cell's minimum and maximum values. In span space, this corresponds to the region:
-$$ x < v \quad \text{AND} \quad y > v $$
-This defines a rectangular region in the 2D span space bounded by $x=v$ and $y=v$ [S1].
-
-### Data Structure
-To perform the range query efficiently, the points in span space are organized using a spatial indexing structure, typically a kd-tree [S1]. This structure allows the algorithm to traverse the space and test only one of the two extreme values (min or max) at each node, pruning branches that cannot contain intersecting cells [S1].
-
+For a given isovalue $v$, a cell intersects the isosurface if and only if $x < v$ and $y > v$ [S1]. This defines a rectangular region in the 2D span space bounded by $x = v$ and $y = v$ [S1].
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### NOISE Algorithm
-The primary implementation of span space decomposition introduced by Livnat et al. It overlays a kd-tree on the span space points to accelerate seed set construction. It is chosen when preprocessing time must be minimized for large datasets like MRI or CT scans, though it may still generate large seed sets for noisy data. See [NOISE Algorithm](1751-noise-algorithm.md).
